class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<vector<int>> res;
        if(root==NULL) return res;
        queue<TreeNode*> q;
        q.push(root);
        bool flag=true;
        while(!q.empty()){
            int n=q.size();
            vector<int> row(n);
            for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
                TreeNode* node=q.front();
                q.pop();
                int index=(flag) ? i:(n-i-1);
                row[index]=node->val;
                if(node->left) q.push(node->left);
                if(node->right) q.push(node->right);
            }
            flag=!flag;
            res.push_back(row);
        }
        return res;
    }
};
Explanation:
The function returns the nodes of a binary tree in zigzag level order.
vector<vector<int>> res; stores the final answer.
If root == NULL, return an empty vector because the tree has no nodes.
A queue<TreeNode*> is used to perform Level Order Traversal (BFS).
The root node is pushed into the queue.
flag = true means traverse the current level from left to right.
flag = false means traverse the current level from right to left.
while(!q.empty()) processes the tree until all nodes are visited.
n = q.size() gives the number of nodes in the current level.
vector<int> row(n) creates a vector to store all node values of the current level.
The for loop runs n times to process every node in that level.
node = q.front() gets the front node from the queue.
q.pop() removes the processed node from the queue.
If flag is true, index = i, so values are stored from left to right.
If flag is false, index = n - i - 1, so values are stored from right to left.
row[index] = node->val places the node's value at the correct position.
If the current node has a left child, it is pushed into the queue.
If the current node has a right child, it is also pushed into the queue.
After completing one level, flag = !flag changes the traversal direction for the next level.
The completed row is added to res, and after all levels are processed, res is returned.
Time Complexity
O(N) — Every node is visited exactly once.
Space Complexity
O(N) — Queue and result vector together can store up to all nodes in the tree.
